/*
	RMSTEST 14 — do the DEFAULT tile budgets scale with map size?

	preview-design Sec.15 item 7a. The guide gives elevation's default as
	"about 120 on tiny maps" (guide:1228) and terrain's as "default:122 tiles on
	a tiny map" (guide:1616). Sec.6.2 and Sec.6.4 model both as scaling with
	area — `120 × dim²/14400` and `122 × dim²/14400`. That "on a tiny map"
	phrasing is suggestive but it is not a statement that the default scales,
	and the modelling has always been flagged as an assumption.

	If the defaults are FIXED rather than scaled, every map larger than Tiny
	gets far less default elevation and terrain than the spec predicts — on
	Ludicrous the error would be 16x.

	ONE MAP, BOTH ANSWERS. Neither create_elevation nor create_terrain declares
	number_of_tiles, so both fall back to their defaults. They live in different
	sections and are read from different histograms, so a single export measures
	both at once.

	RUN IT TWICE: once on TINY (120x120 = 14400), once on SMALL
	(144x144 = 20736). Two seeds each is worth it — both quantities are GROWN,
	so each carries the ~3% overshoot and about 2% run-to-run spread measured in
	RMSTEST_13.

	READ IT WITH THE PROBE
	  elevation default = the count at level 1
	  terrain default   = the DIRT (6) count

	COMPARE THE RATIO, NOT THE ABSOLUTE VALUES. Growth overshoot inflates both
	sizes by the same ~3%, so it cancels in a ratio and would otherwise muddy a
	direct comparison against 120 or 122.

	  Small/Tiny ≈ 1.44  -> the defaults scale with area (20736/14400 = 1.44).
	                        Sec.6.2/6.4's `× dim²/14400` modelling is right.
	  Small/Tiny ≈ 1.00  -> the defaults are FIXED. Both sections are wrong for
	                        every size above Tiny, and the constants become
	                        literal 120/122 with no scaling term.
	  anything else      -> report both raw numbers; a third relationship
	                        (side-length rather than area, say, which would give
	                        1.2) is worth knowing about.

	Sanity anchor: on Tiny the raw numbers should land near 124 and 126 — the
	guide's 120/122 plus overshoot. If Tiny itself is far off those, the guide's
	stated defaults are wrong and that matters more than the scaling question.

	DESIGN NOTES
	  MaxHeight 1 so all raised tiles sit at level 1, one histogram bucket.
	  One clump each, so no budget division.
	  DIRT is the terrain because it is visually and numerically distinct from
	  the GRASS base and is already a known constant (id 6).
	  Player lands are tiny and pushed to the rim; elevation avoids player-land
	  origins by about 9 tiles (guide:1169) and could otherwise eat the budget.
	  Sections run LAND -> ELEVATION -> TERRAIN (guide:135), so the dirt is
	  painted after the hill exists. That does not disturb the elevation count,
	  which lives on a separate layer from terrain.

	RUN ON: Tiny, then Small. 2 players.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain GRASS

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 45 0
}

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

create_elevation 1
{
	base_terrain GRASS
	number_of_clumps 1
}

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

create_terrain DIRT
{
	base_terrain GRASS
	number_of_clumps 1
}
